                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_smatrix_theories_and_string_theory_2"} In S-matrix theories and string theory
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A basic characteristic of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+relativity"} special relativity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            is that causal influences on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            propagate in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/timelike"} timelike
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
            directions but not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
          %p
            The fact that any two spacelike-separated regions of spacetime thus behave like
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics#Subsystems"} independent subsystems
            is called
            %strong causal locality
            or, with a slightly stronger technical definition,
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Einstein causality
          %p
            (One sometimes sees a further criterion to causality, that the causal influences in timelike and lightlike directions only propagate into the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/future"} future
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entropy"} entropy
            in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+physics"} statistical physics
          %p
            From (
            %a{:href => "#Grigoriev"}> Grigor’ev 197x
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p Microcausality condition
            %p
              a requirement that the causality condition (which states that cause must precede effect) be satisfied down to an arbitrarily small distance and time interval. The microcausality condition usually refers to distances ≲
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 16
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{-16}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meter"} cm
              and to times ≲
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 24
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{-24}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second"} sec
            %p
              It is shown in the theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+relativity"} relativity
              that the assumption of the existence of physical signals that propagate with a velocity greater than the velocity of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light"} light
              leads to violation of the causality requirement. Thus, the microcausality condition prohibits the propagation of signals at a velocity greater than the velocity of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light"} light
              “in the small”.
            %p
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operator"} operators
              correspond to physical quantities, the microcausality condition requires the interchangeability of any operators that pertain to two points of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space-time"} space-time
              if these points cannot be linked by a light signal. This interchangeability means that the physical quantities to which these operators correspond can be precisely determined independently and simultaneously. The microcausality condition is important in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} axiomatic approaches
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
              concepts of interaction and therefore can be used for direct verification of the microcausality condition. In the most highly developed branch of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              —
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
              — the microcausality condition has been experimentally verified for distances ≲
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 15
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{-15}
              cm (and, correspondingly, for times ≲
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 25
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{-25}
              sec).
            %p The violation of the microcausality condition would make it necessary to radically alter the method of describing physical processes and to reject the dynamic description used in modern theories, in which the state of a physical system at a given moment of time (the effect) is determined by the states of the system at preceding times (the cause).
          %p
            Notice that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 15
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{-15}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cm"} cm
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 17
                  %mi m
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} = 10^{-17}m  = 10^{-2}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fm"} fm
            and that the (charge)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proton"} proton
            is about 0.8
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fm"} fm
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Grigoriev"} Grigor’ev 197x
          %p
            It seems that
            %a{:href => "#Grigoriev"} Grigor’ev 197x
            just cited the length scale resolution of particle accelerators at that time. More recently, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            (see there) probes scales
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 20
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq 10^{-20}m
          %h2#InAlgebraicQuantumFieldTheory In algebraic quantum field theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
            causal locality is formalized as follows. This is a key statement in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+local+nets+of+quantum+observables"} causally local nets of quantum observables
          #CausalLocalityOfAlgebrasOfObservables.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong (causal locality of algebras of observables)
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-presheaf"} co-presheaf
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+quantum+observables"} algebras of quantum observables
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              on some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              is
              %em causally local
              if the algebras
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})
              localized in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
              separated
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              regions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
              commute with each other (inside any of the algebras of observables localized in the causal closure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
              of the union of the two spacetime regions).
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mtext spacelike separated from
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mn 2
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    \\mathcal{O}_1
                    \\;\text{spacelike separated from}
                    \\;
                    \\mathcal{O}2
                    \\right)
                    \\;\Rightarrow\;
                    \\left(
                    [\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_1), \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_2)]
                    \\;=\;
                    0
                    \\;\;\;\;
                    \\in \mathcal{A}( \mathcal{O} )
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          %p
            Under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"} Wick rotation
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Osterwalder-Schrader+theorem"} Osterwalder-Schrader theorem
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
            this condition follows from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+additivity"} causal additivity
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            (see there the section
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#CausalLocality"} Causal locality and Quantum obsrvables
          %p There are variants that one may consider:
          #StrongLocality.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (strong causal locality of algebras of observables)
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+quantum+observables"} local net of quantum observables
              is
              %strong strongly causally local
              if it is causally local in that algebras
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 = A(O_1)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_2 = A(O_2)
              associated with spacelike separated regions commute with each other, and in addition for all commutative subalgebras
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \subset A_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2 \subset A_2
              the algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∨
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∨
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \vee C_2 \subset A(O_1 \vee O_2)
              satisfies
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∨
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∩
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 1
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C_1 \vee C_2) \cap A_1 = C_1
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∨
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∩
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C_1 \vee C_2) \cap A_2 = C_2
                  \.
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"} Nuiten 2011, def. 14
          %p There have been various proposals to understand these conditions from other principles:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a{:href => "#Schreiber09"} Schreiber 2009
                the condition
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CausalLocalityOfAlgebrasOfObservables"}
                is related to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-functor"} n-functoriality
                of a corresponding (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"}> Schrödinger picture
                )
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+quantum+field+theory"} functorial quantum field theory
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"} Nuiten 2011, theorem 4.2
                the condition
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StrongLocality"}
                us shown to be implied by the associated pre-sheaf of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"} Bohr toposes
                satisfying spatial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
                by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+geometric+morphisms"} local geometric morphisms
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagenImaniRejzner12"} Brunetti, Fredenhagen, Imani &amp; Rejzner 2012
                condition def.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CausalLocalityOfAlgebrasOfObservables"}
                is shown to be equivalent to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-presheaf"} co-presheaf
                of observables being a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
                is a suitable way.
          %h2#in_smatrix_theories_and_string_theory In S-matrix theories and string theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+additivity"} causal additivity
            is meant to also be incarnated in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+function"} analyticity
            properties of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            (for this reason one often speaks of “the analytic S-matrix”).
          %p
            One S-matrix theory is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            %a{:href => "#Martinec95"} Martinec 1995
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ErlerGross04"} Erler &amp; Gross 2004
          %blockquote
            %p
              Perhaps then it comes as a surprise that critical string theory produces an analytic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              consistent with macroscopic causality. In absence of any other known theoretical mechanism which might explain this, despite appearances one is lead to believe that string interactions must be, in some sense, local.
          %p and
          %blockquote
            %p We find that string theory avoids problems with nonlocality in a surprising way. In particular, we find that the Witten vertex is “local enough” to allow for a nonsingular description of the theory which is completely local along a single null direction.
          %p and
          %blockquote
            %p unlike lightcone string field theory, it is clear that cubic string field theory at least has a local limit where all spacetime coordinates are taken to the midpoint. We investigate this limit with a careful choice of regulator and show that at any stage the theory is nonsingular but arbitrarily close to being local and manifestly causal. We believe that the existence of this limit, though singular, must account for the macroscopic causality of the string S-matrix. Thus, string theory is local enough to avoid the inconsistencies of a theory which is acausal and nonlocal in time, but is nonlocal enough to make string theory different from quantum field theory
          %p Then they comment on Martinec’s account above, and other’s, by saying:
          %blockquote
            %p To motivate our particular perspective, it seems appropriate to discuss earlier attempts to understand the role of locality, causality and time in string theory, and explain why we feel these approaches do not adequately address the problems just raised.
